    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under LIFO, when inventory quantities drop below prior-year levels, older, lower-cost LIFO layers are released into cost of goods sold, replacing the current-period higher replacement costs that would otherwise have been charged to COGS. This is a LIFO liquidation. The income effect is the difference between the replacement cost of the units sold from older layers and the historical cost at which those layers were carried. The P&L impact flows through a reduced COGS, which increases gross profit and operating margin — but the gain is embedded in COGS, not presented as a separate line item. Regarding disclosure: ASC 270-10-50-6 requires interim financial statements to disclose material events or transactions that affect the comparability of interim results. A LIFO liquidation that materializes from a non-recurring event (strike) and significantly distorts reported operating margin meets this threshold because users comparing Q3 Year 2 to Q3 Year 1 would see an inflated margin that does not reflect sustainable operating performance. The SEC also requires disclosure of material LIFO liquidations (Regulation S-X, Rule 5-02-6(b)). The appropriate disclosure is a note quantifying the LIFO liquidation effect on cost of goods sold and net income, allowing financial statement users to distinguish operating results from the non-recurring inventory liquidation gain. A critical exam trap is confusing separate income statement presentation (not required — the gain is embedded in COGS) with note disclosure (required if material). Another trap is treating the Q4 replenishment as automatically reversing the Q3 income effect — replacement at current prices creates a new LIFO layer at current cost; the Q3 lower COGS is recognized in Q3 regardless of subsequent replenishment.",
