    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 842-10-25-2, a lease is classified as a finance lease if any one of five criteria is met at lease commencement: (a) transfer of ownership by end of term, (b) purchase option that the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise, (c) lease term for the major part (typically 75% or more) of the remaining economic life, (d) present value of lease payments and any residual value guarantee equals or exceeds substantially all (typically 90%) of the fair value of the underlying asset, or (e) the asset is so specialized it has no alternative use to the lessor. The bargain purchase option is dispositive here. The exercise price is $850,000 while the expected fair value at year 10 is $1,800,000 — Cascade can buy an asset worth $1.8 million for $850,000, a bargain that creates a significant economic incentive to exercise. Under ASC 842-10-25-2(b), existence of a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise results in finance lease classification. The deputy's analysis incorrectly focuses on the lease term test (criterion c) while ignoring criterion b — the bargain purchase option alone triggers finance lease classification regardless of whether other criteria are met. Once the option is determined to be a bargain, ASC 842-10-30-1 requires the lease payments to include the exercise price of the purchase option. The lease liability (and initial ROU asset) equals the present value of the 10 annual payments ($2,009,000) plus the present value of the bargain purchase option exercise price ($453,000) = $2,462,000. An exam trap is confusing the lease term for classification purposes: the classification test considers whether a purchase option is reasonably certain of exercise (criterion b), not whether the stated lease term exceeds 75% of economic life (criterion c). Once criterion b is satisfied, finance lease classification is required regardless of what the lease-term test shows. Another trap: because the purchase option is a bargain, the option exercise price is included in lease payments for measurement of the lease liability — this is not optional.",
