    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 810-10-25-38A, the VIE consolidation model applies when an entity is a variable interest entity (VIE). Axion meets the VIE criteria because its equity at risk ($2,800,000) is insufficient to finance its activities without additional subordinated financial support — a classic VIE indicator under ASC 810-10-15-14(b)(1). The voting interest model is bypassed entirely when a reporting entity holds a variable interest in a VIE; the VIE model is the exclusive framework for determining consolidation. Under the VIE model, the primary beneficiary is the party that has both: (1) the power to direct the activities of the VIE that most significantly impact the VIE's economic performance [ASC 810-10-25-38A(a)], and (2) the obligation to absorb losses or the right to receive benefits that could potentially be significant to the VIE [ASC 810-10-25-38A(b)]. Ridgeview satisfies both conditions: (a) Power criterion — Ridgeview's CFO sits on the 5-member board with veto power over the operating budget, major capital expenditures, and CEO compensation. These veto rights allow Ridgeview to direct Axion's most significant economic activities. The manufacturing services agreement further demonstrates that Axion operates primarily for Ridgeview's benefit. (b) Loss-absorption criterion — Ridgeview's $4,500,000 loan guarantee exposes Ridgeview to absorb Axion's expected losses. This guarantee exceeds Axion's equity at risk ($2,800,000), making it significant to the VIE. The general counsel's argument errs by applying the voting interest model to a VIE — the ownership percentage (45%) is irrelevant under the VIE framework. An exam trap is assuming the voting interest model applies whenever ownership is below 50%. Once an entity qualifies as a VIE, the VIE model supersedes the voting interest model regardless of ownership percentages. Another trap: confusing 'significant influence' (equity method threshold) with 'controlling financial interest' (consolidation threshold) — the VIE model uses different criteria entirely.",
